## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of branchless, scalable bit-twiddling techniques for 64-bit inputs.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR style bit reversal
- Masking and shifting sequences
- Branchless algorithms for performance

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>

uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t n) {
  n = ((n >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1);
  n = ((n >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2);
  n = ((n >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4);
  n = ((n >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8);
  n = ((n >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((n & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16);
  n = (n >> 32) | (n << 32);
  return n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for smaller word sizes or specialized ISAs?
- How would you adapt for 128-bit inputs and performance considerations?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of 64-bit bit reversals and cross-half manipulation without loops. It ensures ability to compose operations on 128-bit types.

## Key Concepts
- 64-bit bit reversal with masks
- 128-bit struct handling
- Non-branching composition

## Code Example
```javascript
// Outline only: reverse64 implemented with a sequence of mask/shifts
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1 | (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2 | (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  x = (x & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL) << 4 | (x >> 4) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  x = ((x & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL);
  x = (x << 48) | ((x & 0xffff) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0xffff);
  return x;
}
```

